From 185ad530e1ef81302c5d93154a484c2a692ce60d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 09:32:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] libroot/malloc: thread caches keep freed blocks as the
 program left them

The thread caches of 0138 linked cached blocks through their first word, as
glibc's tcache does. Haiku's allocators never wrote into a freed block until
it was reused, and programs have come to depend on that without knowing it:
Slayer deletes the thread items of a list, then dynamic_casts every entry of
the same list, deleted ones included. With the old allocator the cast read an
intact vtable pointer and returned NULL; with 0138 it read the free list's
link and crashed, at every refresh of its window.

A class is now an array of pointers in the thread's cache, as jemalloc's
thread caches are. A freed block is not written to until it is handed out
again; the cache hands out the most recently freed block first and gives its
oldest half back when a class is full. free() no longer touches the block's
cache line either, which helps most when another thread allocated it: frees
from other threads went from 16-35 to 45-61 million a second with one to four
producer/consumer pairs.

A second free() of the block last freed in its class is still stopped at
once; other double frees are caught when the cache gives the block back.

Tests: HaikuArmQemu's tools/malloc/malloctest 10/10; Haiku's calloc_test,
memalign_test, signal_in_allocator_test[2]; Slayer runs (it crashed within
seconds on 0138). The Mojo compile is unchanged (2.9 s -j 1, 3.7 s -j 8).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
